WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.890
Terrain changes altitude relationships and can hide the aircraft, create wind effects,

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.886
or reduce emergency landing options.

00:00:06.886 --> 00:00:10.479
Plan clearance relative to the actual surface and obstacles,

00:00:10.479 --> 00:00:12.874
not only an abstract launch elevation.

00:00:12.874 --> 00:00:17.265
Common hazards include trees, branches, wires, towers, antennas, buildings, cranes, vehicles,

00:00:17.265 --> 00:00:18.862
people, and temporary equipment.

00:00:18.862 --> 00:00:25.249
Thin wires and small branches can be difficult for both pilots and obstacle sensors to detect.

00:00:25.249 --> 00:00:29.240
Launch and recovery surfaces should be stable, clear, and controlled.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.430
Dust, loose debris, snow, water, slope, or vegetation can affect motors, sensors, payloads,

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:35.627
and crew footing.

00:00:35.627 --> 00:00:38.022
Select alternate landing areas before takeoff.

00:00:38.022 --> 00:00:41.615
Structures can block sky view and distort satellite-navigation signals.

00:00:41.615 --> 00:00:43.212
Urban canyons, overhangs, cliffs,

00:00:43.212 --> 00:00:47.603
and dense vegetation may reduce position accuracy or create reflected signals.

00:00:47.603 --> 00:00:52.393
A stable position-hold display should not be treated as a permanent guarantee.

00:00:52.393 --> 00:00:57.183
Obstacle-avoidance systems have blind spots and performance limits related to size, texture,

00:00:57.183 --> 00:00:59.578
lighting, speed, direction, and sensor cleanliness.

00:00:59.578 --> 00:01:03.969
They assist the operator but do not replace visual awareness, clearance,

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:05.566
or conservative path planning.

00:01:05.566 --> 00:01:09.957
Maintain a simple route with sufficient space and an escape direction.

00:01:09.957 --> 00:01:14.747
If people, vehicles, equipment, or conditions change, pause or end the mission.

00:01:14.747 --> 00:01:18.340
A closer view is not worth losing safe clearance.
